INT. ICE-FISHING HUT - NIGHT

A plywood box on a frozen lake. A propane heater glows orange. A hole in the floor shows black water. A radio hangs on a nail.

GRETA KOWALCZYK (40s, wool cap, thermos) sits on an upturned bucket, jigging a line. She does not look at the radio.

Wind presses at the walls. The heater ticks.

The radio crackles.

RUTH (V.O.)
(over radio, faint)
Shanty nine. Greta. Are you there?

Greta's jigging hand stops. She waits. The radio hisses.

RUTH (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(over radio)
I know you can hear me. You always keep it on.

Greta reels in. Empty hook. She baits it again, slowly. Her thermos lid rattles against its cup.

RUTH (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(over radio)
The ice is thinning by the east shore. Come in before dark.

GRETA
(flat)
It's dark.

RUTH (V.O.)
(over radio)
Then come in now.

Greta takes the handset off its hook. She does not press the key. She holds it against her chest.

A sound like a rifle shot rolls across the lake. The hut lurches a quarter inch. Greta grips the bucket rim.

RUTH (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(over radio, closer)
Greta. Listen to me. I'm not at the station.

Greta lowers her eyes to the hole in the floor. The black water is still. Something taps the ice beneath it. Once. Twice.

GRETA
(whisper)
Where are you, then?

The radio hisses. The tapping stops. Then, from the hole, in a voice that is exactly the radio's:

RUTH (V.O.)
(from the water)
Here.
